geky/gf256 v0.3.1 - https://github.com/geky/gf256
Copyright (c) 2021, Christopher Haster. All rights reserved.
BSD-3-Clause.

The GF(2^8) arithmetic and Shamir split/reconstruction structure in
api/rust/custody-core were adapted from the upstream implementation. Horizon
does not link or ship the upstream crate as a runtime dependency: the required
operations are maintained in-tree and have been adapted for fail-closed input
validation, caller-supplied OS randomness, constant-time field arithmetic,
zeroization and locked memory. The field implementation is checked against
reference arithmetic over all 65 536 operand pairs and all 255 non-zero
inverses, in addition to Rust/Python parity tests, property tests, fuzzing and
compiled-output inspection. These controls do not constitute certification of
the implementation.

The Shamir scheme itself was published by Adi Shamir in "How to Share a
Secret", Communications of the ACM 22(11), 1979,
https://doi.org/10.1145/359168.359176.

